# Every creature step (Map::moveCreature: two queries and a union), magic effect, distance effect, health bar and area
# spell asks Map::getSpectators. The sector walk is cheap; what the stock code paid around it was memory and a quadratic
# union:
#   * SpectatorVec reserved 32 pointers on the heap in its constructor: one allocation (and free) for every caller, used
#     or not (a few hundred per second of hunting per player in view).
#   * the spectator cache was a std::map<Position, SpectatorVec>: a tree node and a vector copy per store, all freed again at
#     the next creature added to or removed from any tile (Tile::addThing / removeThing / internalAddThing clear it), so in
#     a hunting pack every query stored an entry the next step threw away.
#   * SpectatorVec::addSpectators (the union of the old and new position of a step, of a distance effect) scanned the whole
#     first list for every element of the second: n * m compares, 2500 for a pack of 45 and 70000 for a crowd of 260.
#   * Map::moveCreature grew a std::vector of stack positions one push_back at a time (4 to 7 allocations per step).
# Now:
#   * src/chaosspectators.h (new, header only, no engine types; server/tools/chaosspectators_test.cpp and
#     server/tools/spectator_bench.cpp build it alone): ChaosSpec::SmallVec (48 pointers inline, heap beyond that),
#     ChaosSpec::SpectatorList (the stock SpectatorVec interface on it; spectators.h aliases SpectatorVec to it),
#     ChaosSpec::appendMissing (the same union, same order, a pointer set when both lists are long) and ChaosSpec::Cache
#     (open addressing, generation stamped slots over a pool of vectors that are reused: clear() is O(1) and frees nothing).
#   * map.h / map.cpp: the two caches are ChaosSpec::Cache; getSpectators looks up and stores by a packed position key;
#     the stack positions of moveCreature are a SmallVec.
# Same behavior on purpose: what a cache holds, when it is looked up, stored and cleared is exactly as before, so every
# query returns the same creatures in the same order (the benchmark's --check runs both on the same random operations and
# compares every answer). Nothing that decides when a query is made changed. Not done: skipping the clear when nothing in
# range changed. The leaf lists remove by swap with the last element, so a retained entry could differ in order from a fresh
# walk, and in a pack everything is in range of everything.
